ORGO.reg({"types":{"e2-stereo-explain":{"id":"e2-stereo-explain","title":"Explain a slow or fast E2 from the chair","exam":"Q5B","points":20,"asks":"One stereoisomer eliminates far more slowly (or faster) than the others. Explain in detail.","know":[{"h":"<b>The requirement.</b> In {{E2}} the hydrogen and the leaving group must be <b>anti-periplanar</b>. The C-H electrons push in from behind the C-LG bond, like the back-side attack of {{SN2}}. This gives the best orbital overlap, the most stable transition state and the fastest reaction.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 2 to 3"},{"h":"<b>Syn-periplanar</b> (H and leaving group on the same side) has poorer overlap and a less stable transition state, so it does not compete.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 3"},{"h":"<b>On a cyclohexane, anti-periplanar means trans-diaxial</b>: an axial leaving group and an axial hydrogen on the carbon next to it.","from":"FA24 Exam 2, Q5B"},{"h":"<b>An equatorial leaving group has no anti hydrogen.</b> Anti to an equatorial bond lies a C-C bond of the ring. Both hydrogens on the next carbon are gauche to it.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 2; standard"},{"h":"<b>The ring flip</b> exchanges axial and equatorial on every carbon at once. It never changes which face (up or down) a group is on.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 1"},{"h":"<b>Trans neighbours</b> are both axial or both equatorial. <b>Cis neighbours</b> are one axial and one equatorial. So a hydrogen on C2 can be anti to a leaving group on C1 only if that hydrogen is trans to the leaving group, which means the substituent on C2 is cis to it.","from":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A and Q5B"},{"h":"<b>Axial groups cost energy</b>: methyl 1.74 kcal/mol (K = 19.5), ethyl 1.79, isopropyl 2.61 (K = 86.0), tert-butyl 5.5 (K = 11,916). K is the ratio of the equatorial chair to the axial chair.","from":"Lecture 10/27, Table 5.3"},{"h":"<b>The rate follows the reactive chair.</b> Only molecules in the chair with the leaving group axial (and an axial H next door) can react. If that chair is the stable one, {{E2}} is fast. If it is rare, {{E2}} is slow. If it does not exist, {{E2}} hardly happens at all.","from":"FA24 Exam 2, Q5B; standard"},{"h":"<b>A tert-butyl group locks the ring</b> with itself equatorial: about 1 molecule in 12,000 has it axial. A leaving group cis to a 4-tert-butyl group is held axial; a leaving group trans to it is held equatorial.","from":"Lecture 10/27, Table 5.3"},{"h":"<b>The exam example.</b> The all-trans isomer of 1,2,3,4,5,6-hexachlorocyclohexane has every Cl equatorial. After the flip every Cl is axial but every H is equatorial. Neither chair has an axial Cl next to an axial H, so it reacts 7000 times more slowly than any other stereoisomer.","from":"FA24 Exam 2, Q5B"},{"h":"<b>Stereoisomers share everything else</b>: same base, same leaving group, same kind of carbon. So the explanation of a rate difference between them is always the geometry, shown on the chair.","from":"standard"},{"h":"<b>Skill: a full-credit answer has four parts.</b> State the anti-periplanar (trans-diaxial) requirement, draw the chair or chairs, say what is anti to the leaving group in each, and compare with the other isomer.","from":"FA24 Exam 2, Q5B rubric"}],"method":["Write the requirement first: H and leaving group anti-periplanar, which on a ring means trans-diaxial on neighbouring carbons.","Draw both chairs of the slow isomer and label the leaving group axial or equatorial in each.","In the chair with the leaving group axial, check the axial position on each neighbouring carbon: hydrogen or substituent?","Decide which case it is: no chair has an axial leaving group next to an axial hydrogen (E2 blocked), or the chair that has them is the unstable one (E2 slow, and say how unstable using the A-values).","Do the same for the fast isomer and point to its axial leaving group and the axial hydrogen next to it.","Finish with one sentence that compares the two isomers."],"pitfalls":[{"h":"Writing only \"steric hindrance\". The reason is the missing anti-periplanar hydrogen, and the chair has to show it."},{"h":"Looking at one chair only. The grader wants to see that the ring flip does not help, or why the flipped chair is rare."},{"h":"Saying the ring flip moves a group to the other face. Up stays up: only axial and equatorial change."},{"h":"Saying an equatorial Cl is too stable to leave. The point is that no hydrogen is anti to it."},{"h":"Explaining the slow isomer and never saying why the other isomers are fast."}],"links":{"read":["e2","rings-2"]}}},"items":{"exam-q5b-written":{"id":"exam-q5b-written","type":"e2-stereo-explain","role":"exam","kind":"written","diff":3,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q5B","time":5,"pts":20,"stem":"This stereoisomer of 1,2,3,4,5,6-hexachlorocyclohexane undergoes {{E2}} elimination about 7000 times more slowly than any of its stereoisomers. Explain why.\n[[chair:1u:Cl,2d:Cl,3u:Cl,4d:Cl,5u:Cl,6d:Cl|B]]","model":"<p>{{E2}} needs the hydrogen and the leaving group <b>anti-periplanar</b>. On a cyclohexane ring that means <b>trans-diaxial</b>: an axial Cl with an axial H on the carbon next to it.</p><p>In the chair drawn, every Cl is equatorial and every H is axial. No Cl is axial, so no Cl has an anti hydrogen.</p>\n[[chair:1u:Cl,2d:Cl,3u:Cl,4d:Cl,5u:Cl,6d:Cl|B]]\n<p>Flipping the ring makes all six Cl axial. That chair is very strained and hardly populated, and it does not help anyway: now every H is equatorial, so there is still no H anti to a Cl.</p>\n[[chair:1u:Cl,2d:Cl,3u:Cl,4d:Cl,5u:Cl,6d:Cl|A]]\n<p>In this isomer every Cl is trans to both of its neighbours, so the H on each neighbouring carbon is always cis to it. Every other stereoisomer has at least one pair of neighbouring Cl that are cis, and so has a chair with an axial Cl next to an axial H. Those isomers eliminate quickly.</p>","rubric":[{"pts":8,"h":"States the requirement: H and Cl anti-periplanar, which on a cyclohexane means trans-diaxial on neighbouring carbons"},{"pts":4,"h":"In the chair drawn every Cl is equatorial, so none has an anti hydrogen"},{"pts":5,"h":"After the ring flip every Cl is axial but every H is equatorial (and that chair is very strained), so there is still no anti H"},{"pts":3,"h":"The other stereoisomers have an axial Cl next to an axial H in one of their chairs, so they react fast"}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Recall what E2 needs","h":"The lecture: the major E2 product comes from the anti-periplanar hydrogen, the most important factor for E2. In a chair, anti-periplanar means both the H and the leaving group are axial, on neighbouring carbons, one up and one down."},{"t":"Read the chair that is drawn","h":"All six Cl are equatorial. The axial positions hold the six hydrogens. An equatorial Cl is anti to ring C-C bonds, not to any hydrogen."},{"t":"Try the ring flip","h":"Now all six Cl are axial. Six axial chlorines cost a great deal of energy, so almost no molecules are in this chair. Worse, the hydrogens are now all equatorial. An axial Cl needs an axial H next door, and there is none."},{"t":"Say why this isomer is the only one","h":"Up and down alternate around the ring: each Cl is trans to both neighbours. If even one neighbouring pair were cis, the chair that makes one of them axial would leave the other equatorial, with an axial H in the right place."}],"answer":"E2 needs an axial Cl with an axial H on the next carbon. In this all-trans isomer neither chair has that: all Cl equatorial in one, all H equatorial in the other. Every other isomer has at least one such pair.","rule":"No trans-diaxial H and leaving group, no easy E2."}}},"mols":{}});
